
(S1) Remark 4.4: the machine 'draw from nu until the answer does not violate R'
    1985 random semimeasures (a tenth of them with v = 1): after T rounds the loop has halted with y with probability nu^R(y)(1 - v^T), exactly; nu^R is a semimeasure that never violates: True
    toy domain of 9 programs: the 81 words r p form a prefix-free set: True; each has one decomposition: True;
    p -> sigma r p is injective for each r: True; length = l(sigma) + l(r) + l(p): True
    normalised probability of y0: 2/3 if machine x halts, 1 if it does not (so '> 2/3' would enumerate the machines that do not halt)

(S2) Theorem 4.11(B) and 4.13 item 4: the constants
    u =    5: W0 = (1/8)(1+1/(2u))^u = 0.20131; empty data: V >= W0 (2u/(2u+1)) / ((17/16) W0 + 2^-6 + 2^-5) = 0.7018
    u =    8: W0 = (1/8)(1+1/(2u))^u = 0.20302; empty data: V >= W0 (2u/(2u+1)) / ((17/16) W0 + 2^-6 + 2^-5) = 0.7277
    u =   16: W0 = (1/8)(1+1/(2u))^u = 0.20452; empty data: V >= W0 (2u/(2u+1)) / ((17/16) W0 + 2^-6 + 2^-5) = 0.7507
    u = 1024: W0 = (1/8)(1+1/(2u))^u = 0.20607; empty data: V >= W0 (2u/(2u+1)) / ((17/16) W0 + 2^-6 + 2^-5) = 0.7748
    (the file's 0.2013 is the value at u = 5; d >= 3 means u >= 8, where W0 >= 0.2030; e^(1/2)/8 = 0.20609)
    (3/4)(16/17)(10/11)(4/5) = 96/187 = 0.51337; non-U weight at most (17/128) e^(1/2) + 1/64 = 0.2346
    4.13 item 4: 0.51/ln 2 = 0.7358 (below 0.74); 0.51337/ln 2 = 0.7406 (at least 0.74); 1/ln 2 = 1.4427 (at most 1.45)
    n* = 1..2000 with d := max{3, least d with 2^d >= 4n*}, c := 5 + n*(d+2): n* <= u/4 and n*(d+2) <= c-5 and d >= 3: True
    without the requirement d >= 3 the choice gives d < 3 for (n*, d) in [(1, 2)]

(S3) Remark 5.5 (rare kinds of input) and the sentence it replaces
    N = 1000, delta = 0.05, pi(R) = 0.5: a_N(R) = 0.00438, 1 - exp(-a_N(R)) = 0.00437, tol_N(R) = 0.04681
    a kind of input of frequency q = 0.02 < tol on which the imitator always gives an answer the human never gives: kl(0 || q) = 0.02020 > a_N(R), so this imitator is not in the matching set
    (b) exact probability that an imitator which differs from the human system only on the input 0 lies in M_N:
    human = function u = 12, N = 3, delta = 0.2, q = 1/u: Pr[Q' in M_N] = 1.0000 >= 1 - delta - N q = 0.5500: True
    human = function u = 30, N = 2, delta = 0.1, q = 1/u: Pr[Q' in M_N] = 1.0000 >= 1 - delta - N q = 0.8333: True
    human = function u = 10, N = 4, delta = 0.3, q = 1/u: Pr[Q' in M_N] = 0.9375 >= 1 - delta - N q = 0.3000: True
    human = coin     u =  8, N = 3, delta = 0.2, q = 1/u: Pr[Q' in M_N] = 0.9841 >= 1 - delta - N q = 0.4250: True
    human = coin     u = 12, N = 2, delta = 0.1, q = 1/u: Pr[Q' in M_N] = 0.9983 >= 1 - delta - N q = 0.7333: True
    the bound of Remark 5.5 holds in all cases: True (it is far from tight in these small cases)

(S4) Lemma 2.8: for which N the hypotheses can be met
    (N+1)^2 <= e^N first at N = 3; (N+1)^3 <= e^N first at N = 6

(S5) Pr2(b) of the note (findings N5 and N6): exact enumeration of the memoriser class, u = 4, K = 12
    n = 0 questions at distinct inputs: at an unseen input Pr[b] = 0.888672 = (2u/(2u+1))/(1+(4u+2)^n/(2^K-1)): True; at a uniform input drawn independently of the data: 0.888672 (the factor (u-n)/u = 1)
    n = 1 questions at distinct inputs: at an unseen input Pr[b] = 0.884999 = (2u/(2u+1))/(1+(4u+2)^n/(2^K-1)): True; at a uniform input drawn independently of the data: 0.663749 (the factor (u-n)/u = 3/4)
    n = 2 questions at distinct inputs: at an unseen input Pr[b] = 0.823716 = (2u/(2u+1))/(1+(4u+2)^n/(2^K-1)): True; at a uniform input drawn independently of the data: 0.411858 (the factor (u-n)/u = 1/2)
    n = 3 questions at distinct inputs: at an unseen input Pr[b] = 0.366677 = (2u/(2u+1))/(1+(4u+2)^n/(2^K-1)): True; at a uniform input drawn independently of the data: 0.091669 (the factor (u-n)/u = 1/4)
    weights proportional to (4u)^-size but of total 1/2 instead of 1 - 2^-K, n = 2: Pr[b] = 0.767472, the formula gives 0.823716
    closure of Corollary 4.6 with C_R = 5: value / value of the open class = 4/5 = 1 - 1/C_R: True
    another closure (the silent copy of the empty table has a thousandth of that share): least C_R = 5000, 1 - 1/C_R = 0.99980, value / value of the open class = 4/5
    formula of Theorem 4.5(b) exact in all four cases: True
